"""Finite queueing, process and inference models. All inputs are illustrative."""
import math
from math_ops import op,OPS

def ar1(p):
 n=int(p[0]);den=n+2*sum((n-k)*p[1]**k for k in range(1,n));return [n*n/den]
op('ar1ess','N Rho','ESS','[p[0]*p[0]/(p[0]+2*Array.from({length:p[0]-1},(_,i)=>(p[0]-i-1)*Math.pow(p[1],i+1)).reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0))]',ar1,'A stationary toy AR(1) sequence has {N} draws and lag-one correlation {Rho}. What finite-sample ESS gives the same variance for its mean?','The calculation sums all lag covariances in this specific AR(1) model. A generic sampler need not have this correlation structure.','grid')
op('tether','Trend Gap','Difference X Y','[p[1],p[0],p[0]+p[1]]',lambda p:[p[1],p[0],p[0]+p[1]],'Two toy series are X=Trend {Trend}, Y=Trend+Gap {Gap}. What is Y-X?','A shared trend can disappear from a difference even as both levels move. This algebra illustrates a tether, not a statistical cointegration test.','grid')
op('braess','Flow Fixed Scale Shortcut','Cost','[p[3]===0?p[1]+p[0]/(2*p[2]):p[0]/p[2]<=p[1]?2*p[0]/p[2]:p[0]/(2*p[2])>=p[1]?p[1]+p[0]/(2*p[2]):2*p[1]]',lambda p:[p[1]+p[0]/(2*p[2]) if p[3]==0 else 2*p[0]/p[2] if p[0]/p[2]<=p[1] else p[1]+p[0]/(2*p[2]) if p[0]/(2*p[2])>=p[1] else 2*p[1]],'Toy traffic Flow {Flow} splits over two symmetric routes, each with one x/Scale {Scale} edge and Fixed {Fixed} edge. Shortcut {Shortcut}=1 joins the two variable edges at zero cost. What is selfish-equilibrium travel cost?','The zero-cost link can change route choices and raise the equilibrium cost. This is the specified nonatomic symmetric network, not a claim about every new road.','graph')
OPS['braess']['graph']='braess'
op('mg1','Arrival Mean Variance','Wait Total Utilization','[p[0]*(p[2]+p[1]*p[1])/(2*(1-p[0]*p[1])),p[1]+p[0]*(p[2]+p[1]*p[1])/(2*(1-p[0]*p[1])),p[0]*p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]*(p[2]+p[1]**2)/(2*(1-p[0]*p[1])),p[1]+p[0]*(p[2]+p[1]**2)/(2*(1-p[0]*p[1])),p[0]*p[1]],'A stable toy M/G/1 queue has Poisson arrival rate {Arrival}, mean service time {Mean}, service-time variance {Variance}. What is mean waiting time before service?','The Pollaczek-Khinchine mean uses both the mean and second moment of service. Stability and the specified arrival/service assumptions are required.','grid')
def erlang(p):
 lam,mu,c,t=p;c=int(c);a=lam/mu;rho=a/c
 term=a**c/math.factorial(c)/(1-rho);cw=term/(sum(a**k/math.factorial(k) for k in range(c))+term);w=cw/(c*mu-lam)
 return [w,w+1/mu,1-cw*math.exp(-(c*mu-lam)*t),rho]
op('erlang','Arrival Service Servers Threshold','Wait Total SLA Utilization','[...(()=>{const a=p[0]/p[1],rho=a/p[2],factor=(k:number)=>Array.from({length:k},(_,i)=>i+1).reduce((x,y)=>x*y,1),term=Math.pow(a,p[2])/factor(p[2])/(1-rho),sum=Array.from({length:p[2]},(_,k)=>Math.pow(a,k)/factor(k)).reduce((x,y)=>x+y,0),c=term/(sum+term),w=c/(p[2]*p[1]-p[0]);return [w,w+1/p[1],1-c*Math.exp(-(p[2]*p[1]-p[0])*p[3]),rho];})()]',erlang,'A stable toy M/M/c queue has arrival rate {Arrival}, service rate {Service} per server, {Servers} servers and wait threshold {Threshold}. What is mean waiting time before service?','Pooling servers changes both waiting probability and delay. Erlang C assumes Poisson arrivals, exponential service, an infinite queue and no abandonment.','grid')
op('staff','Offered Z','Servers','[Math.ceil(p[0]+p[1]*Math.sqrt(p[0]))]',lambda p:[math.ceil(p[0]+p[1]*math.sqrt(p[0]))],'A toy square-root staffing rule uses offered load {Offered} plus Z {Z} times its square root, rounded up. What staffing count results?','This explicit rule scales a safety margin with square-root load. It does not guarantee a particular service level without a queueing model.','grid')
op('little','WIP Throughput','Cycle','[p[0]/p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]/p[1]],'A stable toy system averages WIP {WIP} units and throughput {Throughput} units per time. What mean cycle time follows?','Little’s law relates compatible long-run averages. A transient backlog or mismatched system boundary need not satisfy a one-snapshot estimate.','grid')
op('fluid','Initial Arrival Capacity Time','Backlog','[Math.max(0,p[0]+(p[1]-p[2])*p[3])]',lambda p:[max(0,p[0]+(p[1]-p[2])*p[3])],'A toy fluid queue starts at {Initial}; constant arrival rate {Arrival} and capacity {Capacity} run for Time {Time}. What backlog remains, reflected at zero?','A persistent input-capacity gap accumulates work. This deterministic fluid approximation omits stochastic waiting-time variability.','timeline')
op('joborder','Long Short ShortFirst','MeanWait','[p[2]===1?p[1]/2:p[0]/2]',lambda p:[p[1]/2 if p[2]==1 else p[0]/2],'Two toy jobs arrive together, durations Long {Long}, Short {Short}. ShortFirst {ShortFirst}=1 serves the short job first, 0 the long. What is mean time waiting before service?','The first job waits zero and the second waits for the first. Mean delay and fairness can favor different scheduling rules.','grid')
op('bottleneck','A B C','Capacity','[Math.min(...p)]',lambda p:[min(p)],'Three toy serial stations have capacities {A},{B},{C} units per time, with unlimited buffers. What is the maximum sustainable line throughput?','A line cannot sustainably exceed its slowest station. Speeding a nonbinding station does not remove the bottleneck.','grid')
op('priority','High Low Capacity Time','LowBacklog','[Math.max(0,p[1]-Math.max(0,p[2]-p[0]))*p[3]]',lambda p:[max(0,p[1]-max(0,p[2]-p[0]))*p[3]],'Toy high-priority arrivals use rate {High} of Capacity {Capacity}; low arrivals rate {Low} get the remainder for Time {Time}. Starting empty, what low-priority fluid backlog accumulates?','Priority can reserve nearly all capacity for one class. This fluid model illustrates starvation pressure rather than a full stochastic priority queue.','grid')
op('green','Arrival Saturation Green Time','Backlog','[Math.max(0,p[0]-p[1]*p[2])*p[3]]',lambda p:[max(0,p[0]-p[1]*p[2])*p[3]],'Toy traffic arrives at rate {Arrival}. Saturation flow is {Saturation}, available for Green fraction {Green}; starting empty over Time {Time}, what fluid backlog accumulates?','Green time changes effective capacity. This average-rate calculation omits within-cycle queues and interactions at other intersections.','grid')
op('hawkes','Base Branching','Rate Multiplier','[p[0]/(1-p[1]),1/(1-p[1])]',lambda p:[p[0]/(1-p[1]),1/(1-p[1])],'A stationary toy linear Hawkes model has base rate {Base} and offspring mean Branching {Branching} below 1. What total mean event rate results?','Descendant generations amplify the immigrant rate. A stationary finite mean requires the stated subcritical branching condition.','grid')
op('compoundpoisson','Rate Time Mean Variance','Expected SD','[p[0]*p[1]*p[2],Math.sqrt(p[0]*p[1]*(p[3]+p[2]*p[2]))]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]*p[2],math.sqrt(p[0]*p[1]*(p[3]+p[2]**2))],'Independent toy claims arrive at Poisson Rate {Rate} for Time {Time}; sizes have Mean {Mean}, Variance {Variance}. What is expected total payout?','Both claim-count randomness and size variability enter the total variance. The model assumes independent identically distributed sizes, independent of arrivals.','grid')
op('ou','Mean Start K Sigma Time','Expected Variance','[p[0]+(p[1]-p[0])*Math.exp(-p[2]*p[4]),p[3]*p[3]*(1-Math.exp(-2*p[2]*p[4]))/(2*p[2])]',lambda p:[p[0]+(p[1]-p[0])*math.exp(-p[2]*p[4]),p[3]**2*(1-math.exp(-2*p[2]*p[4]))/(2*p[2])],'A toy Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process has long-run Mean {Mean}, Start {Start}, reversion K {K}, diffusion Sigma {Sigma}. What is expected level at Time {Time}?','The conditional mean returns toward the center while stochastic variance grows toward its stationary level. A mean curve is not a realized sample path.','grid')
op('gbm','Initial Drift Sigma Time','Median Expected','[p[0]*Math.exp((p[1]-p[2]*p[2]/2)*p[3]),p[0]*Math.exp(p[1]*p[3])]',lambda p:[p[0]*math.exp((p[1]-p[2]**2/2)*p[3]),p[0]*math.exp(p[1]*p[3])],'A toy geometric Brownian price starts {Initial}, drift {Drift}, diffusion Sigma {Sigma}, over Time {Time}. What is its median terminal price?','Volatility separates the median from the mean under this lognormal model. The specified drift is an assumption rather than a market forecast.','grid')
op('availability','Break Repair Start Time','Up','[p[1]/(p[0]+p[1])+(p[2]-p[1]/(p[0]+p[1]))*Math.exp(-(p[0]+p[1])*p[3])]',lambda p:[p[1]/(p[0]+p[1])+(p[2]-p[1]/(p[0]+p[1]))*math.exp(-(p[0]+p[1])*p[3])],'A toy two-state continuous-time machine has breakdown rate {Break}, repair rate {Repair}, initial up probability {Start}. What is up probability at Time {Time}?','Repair and failure rates jointly determine availability and transient recovery. Independence and constant hazards are explicit model assumptions.','grid')
op('immigration','Arrival Death Initial Time','Expected','[p[2]*Math.exp(-p[1]*p[3])+p[0]/p[1]*(1-Math.exp(-p[1]*p[3]))]',lambda p:[p[2]*math.exp(-p[1]*p[3])+p[0]/p[1]*(1-math.exp(-p[1]*p[3]))],'A toy population receives immigrants at rate {Arrival}; per-capita death rate {Death}; initial size {Initial}. What is expected size at Time {Time}?','Surviving initial members and surviving immigrants both contribute. The equilibrium mean is a model balance, not a guarantee about one realized population.','grid')
op('bridge','Sigma Time Horizon','Variance','[p[0]*p[0]*p[1]*(p[2]-p[1])/p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]**2*p[1]*(p[2]-p[1])/p[2]],'A zero-endpoint toy Brownian bridge has diffusion Sigma {Sigma} and Horizon {Horizon}. What is its variance at Time {Time}?','Pinning both endpoints removes uncertainty at the ends. The variance profile describes a distribution of paths, not one deterministic trajectory.','curve')
OPS['bridge']['plot']=[1,0,1]
op('mixingtv','A B Start Steps','TV Gap','[Math.abs(p[2]-p[0]/(p[0]+p[1]))*Math.pow(Math.abs(1-p[0]-p[1]),p[3]),1-Math.abs(1-p[0]-p[1])]',lambda p:[abs(p[2]-p[0]/(p[0]+p[1]))*abs(1-p[0]-p[1])**p[3],1-abs(1-p[0]-p[1])],'A toy two-state chain transitions 0→1 with A {A}, 1→0 with B {B}; initial P(1) {Start}. After {Steps} steps, what is total-variation distance from stationarity?','The nontrivial eigenvalue controls the transient distance in this two-state model. A stationary distribution can exist even when periodicity prevents convergence.','grid')
op('balanceflow','A B','Forward Reverse','[p[0]*p[1]/(p[0]+p[1]),p[0]*p[1]/(p[0]+p[1])]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]/(p[0]+p[1])]*2,'A toy two-state chain has transition chances A {A} and B {B}. At stationarity, what probability flow goes 0→1 per step?','The forward and reverse stationary flows agree here. Detailed balance is stronger than merely having an invariant distribution in a general chain.','grid')
op('covercomplete','Nodes','Steps','[(p[0]-1)*Array.from({length:p[0]-1},(_,i)=>1/(i+1)).reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)]',lambda p:[(p[0]-1)*sum(1/i for i in range(1,int(p[0])))],'A toy walk on the complete graph of {Nodes} nodes starts at one node and always moves to a different uniform node. What is expected time to visit all nodes?','Collection becomes slower as fewer unseen nodes remain. This complete-graph formula does not apply to arbitrary network topologies.','grid')
op('normalmh','Current Proposed','Acceptance','[Math.min(1,Math.exp((p[0]*p[0]-p[1]*p[1])/2))]',lambda p:[min(1,math.exp((p[0]**2-p[1]**2)/2))],'A symmetric toy Metropolis proposal moves from {Current} to {Proposed} under a standard-normal target. What is acceptance probability?','Acceptance compares target densities because proposal terms cancel here. High acceptance alone does not establish rapid exploration.','grid')
op('leapfrog','Position Momentum Step','NewPosition EnergyError','[p[0]+p[2]*(p[1]-p[2]*p[0]/2),(()=>{const q=p[0]+p[2]*(p[1]-p[2]*p[0]/2),v=p[1]-p[2]*p[0]/2-p[2]*q/2;return (q*q+v*v-p[0]*p[0]-p[1]*p[1])/2;})()]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[2]*(p[1]-p[2]*p[0]/2),((p[0]+p[2]*(p[1]-p[2]*p[0]/2))**2+(p[1]-p[2]*p[0]/2-p[2]*(p[0]+p[2]*(p[1]-p[2]*p[0]/2))/2)**2-p[0]**2-p[1]**2)/2],'One toy leapfrog step uses harmonic potential q²/2, Position {Position}, Momentum {Momentum}, Step {Step}. What new position results?','A half momentum step, full position step and final half momentum step approximate Hamiltonian motion. The energy error still needs a correction in HMC.','grid')
op('uturn','Start Position Momentum','Dot','[(p[1]-p[0])*p[2]]',lambda p:[(p[1]-p[0])*p[2]],'A one-dimensional toy path starts at {Start}, reaches {Position} with Momentum {Momentum}. What is (position-start)×momentum?','A negative dot product indicates motion back toward the start in this simplified check. Full NUTS uses a trajectory-building and stopping procedure beyond this scalar test.','grid')
op('noncenter','V Z','X SD','[Math.exp(p[0]/2)*p[1],Math.exp(p[0]/2)]',lambda p:[math.exp(p[0]/2)*p[1],math.exp(p[0]/2)],'A toy funnel coordinate is X=exp(V/2)×Z. With V {V}, Z {Z}, what X results?','The same standardized coordinate maps into very different scales across the funnel. Reparameterizing changes geometry without creating new data.','grid')
op('rhat','N Within Between','Rhat','[Math.sqrt(((p[0]-1)/p[0]*p[1]+p[2]/p[0])/p[1])]',lambda p:[math.sqrt(((p[0]-1)/p[0]*p[1]+p[2]/p[0])/p[1])],'A toy classic variance-ratio diagnostic has per-chain N {N}, within-chain variance {Within}, N-scaled between-chain variance {Between}. What square-root ratio results?','This classic summary compares within and between variation. Modern rank-normalized diagnostics and other checks are needed for robust sampler assessment.','grid')
op('swap','ColdBeta HotBeta ColdEnergy HotEnergy','Acceptance','[Math.min(1,Math.exp((p[0]-p[1])*(p[2]-p[3])))]',lambda p:[min(1,math.exp((p[0]-p[1])*(p[2]-p[3])))],'Two toy tempering chains have inverse temperatures {ColdBeta},{HotBeta} and energies {ColdEnergy},{HotEnergy}. What swap acceptance probability follows?','A swap ratio accounts for both temperatures and energies. Hot-chain exploration helps only if information can move between chains.','grid')
op('slice','Height','Width','[2*Math.sqrt(-2*Math.log(p[0]))]',lambda p:[2*math.sqrt(-2*math.log(p[0]))],'For unnormalized toy density exp(-x²/2), choose slice Height {Height} in (0,1). What is the full width of the retained horizontal slice?','Conditioning on a height turns this density level set into an interval. A full slice sampler must also sample its height and position correctly.','curve')
OPS['slice']['plot']=[0,.001,.999]
op('weights','A B C','First ESS','[p[0]/(p[0]+p[1]+p[2]),Math.pow(p[0]+p[1]+p[2],2)/(p[0]*p[0]+p[1]*p[1]+p[2]*p[2])]',lambda p:[p[0]/sum(p),sum(p)**2/sum(x*x for x in p)],'Three positive toy importance weights are {A},{B},{C}. What is the first normalized weight?','Weight concentration reduces the usual importance-weight ESS. This diagnostic differs from autocorrelation-based ESS for an MCMC sequence.','grid')
op('elbo','LogEvidence KL','ELBO Gap','[p[0]-p[1],p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]-p[1],p[1]],'A toy model has log evidence {LogEvidence} and KL gap {KL}. From ELBO=log evidence-KL, what bound value results?','The identity relates approximation quality and the evidence bound. A high numerical objective alone does not guarantee the approximation captures every posterior feature.','grid')
op('laplace','Mode Curvature','Variance SD','[1/p[1],1/Math.sqrt(p[1])]',lambda p:[1/p[1],1/math.sqrt(p[1])],'At toy mode {Mode}, the negative log-posterior has positive curvature {Curvature}. What local Gaussian variance does the Laplace approximation use?','Local curvature gives a Gaussian scale around one mode. Skewness, tails and other modes can make that local approximation inadequate globally.','grid')
op('affineflow','X Scale Shift','Y LogDensity','[p[2]+p[1]*p[0],-p[0]*p[0]/2-Math.log(2*Math.PI)/2-Math.log(Math.abs(p[1]))]',lambda p:[p[2]+p[1]*p[0],-p[0]**2/2-math.log(2*math.pi)/2-math.log(abs(p[1]))],'Transform a standard-normal toy coordinate X {X} by Y=Shift {Shift}+Scale {Scale}×X, with nonzero scale. What Y results?','The density transformation also accounts for the Jacobian. This single affine step illustrates a flow component, not a complete expressive flow model.','grid')
op('reparam','Mean SD Epsilon','Sample GradMean GradSD','[p[0]+p[1]*p[2],1,p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]*p[2],1,p[2]],'A toy normal sample is Mean {Mean}+SD {SD}×fixed noise Epsilon {Epsilon}. What sample results?','Holding the noise draw fixed exposes derivatives with respect to mean and scale. The noise distribution still defines the resulting sample distribution.','grid')
op('gaussiankl','Mean SD','KL','[(p[0]*p[0]+p[1]*p[1]-1-Math.log(p[1]*p[1]))/2]',lambda p:[(p[0]**2+p[1]**2-1-math.log(p[1]**2))/2],'Q is toy Normal({Mean},{SD}²), P standard normal. What is KL(Q||P) in nats?','A Gaussian approximation can differ in location and scale. This exact family comparison does not evaluate approximation error for a non-Gaussian target.','grid')
op('uncertainlinear','WeightMean WeightVar X Noise','Mean Variance','[p[0]*p[2],p[1]*p[2]*p[2]+p[3]]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[2],p[1]*p[2]**2+p[3]],'A toy linear weight has mean {WeightMean}, variance {WeightVar}; input X {X}, independent output-noise variance {Noise}. What predictive mean results?','Weight uncertainty and observation noise contribute differently to predictive variance. This one-weight model is not a full Bayesian neural network.','grid')
op('poolnormal','Grand Group N NoiseVar GroupVar','Posterior Weight','[(p[4]*p[1]+p[3]/p[2]*p[0])/(p[4]+p[3]/p[2]),p[4]/(p[4]+p[3]/p[2])]',lambda p:[(p[4]*p[1]+p[3]/p[2]*p[0])/(p[4]+p[3]/p[2]),p[4]/(p[4]+p[3]/p[2])],'Toy group mean {Group} comes from N {N} observations of variance {NoiseVar}. A normal prior has Grand mean {Grand}, GroupVar {GroupVar}. What posterior mean results?','Small or noisy groups borrow more from the stipulated population prior. Hyperparameter uncertainty is held fixed in this elementary update.','grid')
op('randomeffects','A SEA B SEB TauVar','Mean SE','[((p[0]/(p[1]*p[1]+p[4]))+(p[2]/(p[3]*p[3]+p[4])))/(1/(p[1]*p[1]+p[4])+1/(p[3]*p[3]+p[4])),Math.sqrt(1/(1/(p[1]*p[1]+p[4])+1/(p[3]*p[3]+p[4])))]',lambda p:[(p[0]/(p[1]**2+p[4])+p[2]/(p[3]**2+p[4]))/(1/(p[1]**2+p[4])+1/(p[3]**2+p[4])),math.sqrt(1/(1/(p[1]**2+p[4])+1/(p[3]**2+p[4])))],'Two toy studies estimate {A},{B} with SEs {SEA},{SEB}; assume known between-study variance {TauVar}. What inverse-total-variance weighted mean results?','Between-study variation changes the weights and pooled uncertainty. Estimating that variation and assessing study validity are separate tasks.','grid')
op('rasch','Ability Difficulty','Probability','[1/(1+Math.exp(p[1]-p[0]))]',lambda p:[1/(1+math.exp(p[1]-p[0]))],'A toy Rasch model uses logit probability Ability {Ability} minus Difficulty {Difficulty}. What is the success probability?','Ability and difficulty enter through their difference. Locating both scales from data requires identification constraints and an appropriate response model.','grid')

op('gpkernel','Distance Length Noise Observation Kernel','Mean Variance','[(()=>{const k=p[4]===0?Math.exp(-p[0]*p[0]/(2*p[1]*p[1])):p[4]===1?Math.exp(-Math.abs(p[0])/p[1]):Math.exp(-2*Math.pow(Math.sin(Math.PI*p[0]),2)/(p[1]*p[1]));return k*p[3]/(1+p[2]);})(),(()=>{const k=p[4]===0?Math.exp(-p[0]*p[0]/(2*p[1]*p[1])):p[4]===1?Math.exp(-Math.abs(p[0])/p[1]):Math.exp(-2*Math.pow(Math.sin(Math.PI*p[0]),2)/(p[1]*p[1]));return 1-k*k/(1+p[2]);})()]',lambda p:[_kernel(p)*p[3]/(1+p[2]),1-_kernel(p)**2/(1+p[2])],'A unit-variance zero-mean toy GP has one Observation {Observation}, noise variance {Noise}, Distance {Distance}, Length {Length}. Kernel {Kernel}: 0 RBF, 1 exponential, 2 unit-period periodic. What posterior mean results?','The kernel supplies similarity and posterior uncertainty. This one-observation conditional calculation does not fit kernel parameters or represent a full dataset.','grid')
def _kernel(p):return math.exp(-p[0]**2/(2*p[1]**2)) if p[4]==0 else math.exp(-abs(p[0])/p[1]) if p[4]==1 else math.exp(-2*math.sin(math.pi*p[0])**2/p[1]**2)
op('gpvariance','Distance Length Noise','Variance','[1-Math.exp(-p[0]*p[0]/(p[1]*p[1]))/(1+p[2])]',lambda p:[1-math.exp(-p[0]**2/p[1]**2)/(1+p[2])],'A unit-variance RBF toy GP has one noisy observation. At Distance {Distance}, Length {Length}, noise variance {Noise}, what posterior latent variance results?','Conditional uncertainty is reduced near the observation. The variance is conditional on the stated kernel and fixed hyperparameters.','curve')
OPS['gpvariance']['plot']=[0,0,3]
op('gpevidence','Observation Amplitude Noise','LogDensity','[-0.5*(Math.log(2*Math.PI*(p[1]+p[2]))+p[0]*p[0]/(p[1]+p[2]))]',lambda p:[-.5*(math.log(2*math.pi*(p[1]+p[2]))+p[0]**2/(p[1]+p[2]))],'A single toy GP observation {Observation} is marginally normal with mean zero and variance Amplitude {Amplitude}+Noise {Noise}. What is its log marginal density?','Marginal likelihood trades off fit and predictive spread. A one-point calculation is not evidence that a multi-parameter optimization has a unique good solution.','grid')
op('ucb','Mean SD Beta','Score','[p[0]+p[2]*p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[2]*p[1]],'A toy acquisition rule scores a point by Mean {Mean}+Beta {Beta}×SD {SD}. What score results?','Increasing the uncertainty weight can favor less explored points. A UCB score is a decision rule, not itself a calibrated probability.','grid')
def ei(p):
 z=(p[0]-p[2])/p[1];cdf=(1+math.erf(z/math.sqrt(2)))/2;pdf=math.exp(-z*z/2)/math.sqrt(2*math.pi)
 return [(p[0]-p[2])*cdf+p[1]*pdf]
op('ei','Mean SD Best','Improvement','[(()=>{const z=(p[0]-p[2])/p[1],pdf=(x:number)=>Math.exp(-x*x/2)/Math.sqrt(2*Math.PI);let cdf=0;if(z>=8)cdf=1;else if(z>-8){const n=2048,h=(z+8)/n;let s=pdf(-8)+pdf(z);for(let i=1;i<n;i++)s+=(i%2?4:2)*pdf(-8+i*h);cdf=s*h/3;}return (p[0]-p[2])*cdf+p[1]*pdf(z);})()]',ei,'A toy normal objective at one candidate has Mean {Mean}, SD {SD}; current Best {Best}. What is expected positive improvement over the best?','Expected improvement combines the chance and size of beating the current best. The normal predictive distribution and current best are explicit assumptions.','grid')
op('gpclass','Mean Variance','ApproxProbability','[1/(1+Math.exp(-p[0]/Math.sqrt(1+Math.PI*p[1]/8)))]',lambda p:[1/(1+math.exp(-p[0]/math.sqrt(1+math.pi*p[1]/8)))],'A toy latent normal has Mean {Mean}, Variance {Variance}. Use the stated approximation logistic(mean/sqrt(1+pi×variance/8)). What class probability results?','Integrating over latent uncertainty can soften a plug-in logistic score. This displayed formula is an approximation, not exact GP classification inference.','grid')
op('gpwork','N M','Ratio','[p[0]*p[0]/(p[1]*p[1])]',lambda p:[p[0]**2/p[1]**2],'Use toy work proxies N³ for a dense GP and N×M² for an approximation with N {N}, M {M}. What is the dense-to-approximate work ratio?','Operation-count proxies describe scaling, not measured runtime or approximation accuracy. Inducing-point placement and implementation still matter.','grid')
op('changeline','Intercept Before After Cut X','Prediction','[p[0]+p[1]*Math.min(p[4],p[3])+p[2]*Math.max(0,p[4]-p[3])]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]*min(p[4],p[3])+p[2]*max(0,p[4]-p[3])],'A continuous toy trend starts at Intercept {Intercept}, slope Before {Before} until Cut {Cut}, then slope After {After}. What is its level at X {X}?','A stated changepoint can alter extrapolation. This piecewise mean is only one component of a possible changepoint GP model.','grid')
op('widthvariance','Width UnitVariance','Variance','[p[0]*p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]],'Sum Width {Width} independent zero-mean toy units, each with variance {UnitVariance}. What is total variance?','Variance scaling can keep a growing sum finite. This calculation alone does not prove a network’s infinite-width process limit.','grid')
op('conditionalnormal','Observation ObsVar TargetVar Covariance Noise','Mean Variance','[p[3]*p[0]/(p[1]+p[4]),p[2]-p[3]*p[3]/(p[1]+p[4])]',lambda p:[p[3]*p[0]/(p[1]+p[4]),p[2]-p[3]**2/(p[1]+p[4])],'Two zero-mean toy normal outputs have variances {ObsVar},{TargetVar}, covariance {Covariance}. Observe the first at {Observation} with noise variance {Noise}. What is the conditional target mean?','Cross-covariance allows one output to inform another. The specified joint covariance must be valid and observation noise is assumed independent.','grid')
op('kalman','Mean PriorVar ProcessVar Observation Noise','Posterior Variance Gain','[(()=>{const pvar=p[1]+p[2],k=pvar/(pvar+p[4]);return p[0]+k*(p[3]-p[0]);})(),(p[1]+p[2])*p[4]/(p[1]+p[2]+p[4]),(p[1]+p[2])/(p[1]+p[2]+p[4])]',lambda p:[p[0]+(p[1]+p[2])/(p[1]+p[2]+p[4])*(p[3]-p[0]),(p[1]+p[2])*p[4]/(p[1]+p[2]+p[4]),(p[1]+p[2])/(p[1]+p[2]+p[4])],'A scalar toy filter predicts from Mean {Mean}, PriorVar {PriorVar}, adds ProcessVar {ProcessVar}, then observes {Observation} with noise variance {Noise}. What posterior mean results?','Prediction grows uncertainty; a measurement combines the predicted state and observation according to their uncertainties. This is a scalar linear-Gaussian update.','grid')
op('squarednormal','Mean Variance Linearized','Expected ExactVariance','[p[2]===1?p[0]*p[0]:p[0]*p[0]+p[1],2*p[1]*p[1]+4*p[0]*p[0]*p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]**2 if p[2]==1 else p[0]**2+p[1],2*p[1]**2+4*p[0]**2*p[1]],'A toy normal X has Mean {Mean}, Variance {Variance}; transform Y=X². Linearized {Linearized}=1 plugs in the mean, 0 uses the exact moment. What predicted mean results?','Curvature can make transforming the mean differ from averaging the transformed variable. This moment example isolates one limitation of linearization.','grid')
op('twolatent','Angle Light','X Y Brightness','[Math.cos(p[0]),Math.sin(p[0]),p[1]]',lambda p:[math.cos(p[0]),math.sin(p[0]),p[1]],'A toy decoder maps Angle {Angle} to (cos(angle),sin(angle)) and keeps brightness Light {Light} separately. What x-coordinate results?','The decoder explicitly separates two controls by construction. Discovering disentangled factors from unconstrained observations is a different inference problem.','grid')
